Kirstie

It is not clear to me that you should be using t-test comparisons.  The post (from 2006) from which you borrowed your calculations was based upon a design in which there were 87 sites:  66 in one management type and 21 in a different management type.

I infer that your situation is different, you mention that you have two sites.  In that case, a simple z-test of the two density estimates will suffice.  However, there is not a rationale for replacement of "se" for "cv".

Indeed this is a post from the archives (2006).  Here's my take on this:

I cannot find mention in Buckland et al. (2001) of the second calculation formed by using difference of log(densities).  When you find yourself with large CVs, the probability of finding differences in densities, by definition, becomes small.

