Sergio

You appear to be doing a very thorough analysis of your data.  You will note that the point estimates of the 11 models you present span a range 220-260; contrast that range with the confidence interval from any single estimate and you will see the point estimates are robust (insensitive) to choice of model.  That is a common property of "good" distance sampling data.

Regarding your question of whether to remove some models before performing model averaging; I would suggest "no".  You (presumably) created your candidate model set a priori.  Live with that decision.  I accept that I have more uncertainty in my estimates that I would wish.

As an aside, my personal preference (other analysts have other preferences) is to not use adjustment terms when including covariates in the detection function.  My belief is that adjustment terms and covariates are performing similar tasks; explaining patterns in detectability not explained by the key function.

